### read

Read one file: text, PDF, image, or notebook. Relative paths resolve against the working directory.
- Text comes back as `12:content`: line number, a colon, then the text. Lines are returned whole, however long they are. The prefix is not file content; strip it when you quote text back into an edit. `show_line_numbers: false` returns the text without prefixes.
- A read stops after 2000 lines and ends with a footer naming the exact `range` that continues it. Use `range` with `start_line`/`end_line` for a slice of the file.
- A whole-file read (no `range`) of a file larger than 262144 bytes is refused with a suggested ranged call; ranged reads are not subject to that cap.
- `range` also takes a byte range, `start_byte` (0-based) and optional `end_byte` (one past the last byte), for text with few or no line breaks: it returns raw text without line prefixes, one page at a time, snapped to UTF-8 character boundaries, and names the offset to continue from. Use it to page through a saved result file.
- Pass either a line range or a byte range, never both.
- Reading the same lines of an unchanged file again, while the earlier result is still in the conversation, returns a short note naming the read call that showed them.
- A Jupyter notebook (.ipynb) read without a range comes back as its cells, each with its id, type, source and a summary of its outputs; a line or byte range reads its raw JSON.
- A PDF comes back as extracted text (via `pdftotext`).
- Directories, pipes and other special files are refused; use `fs_search` to find files.
- Reading records which lines you have seen: `patch` and an overwriting `write` require a prior read of the file, and `patch` requires that every line it changes was shown.
- You can call multiple tools in a single response; read several files in parallel when you need them.

---

### write

Create a file, or replace one whole. Missing parent directories are created.
- Replacing an existing file needs `overwrite=true` and a prior `read` of the file in this conversation; one page is enough, the whole file is not needed. Without `overwrite=true` an existing file is refused.
- If the file changed on disk since that read, the overwrite is refused and nothing is written; the error names both hashes and shows the diff from what you read to what is there now, and a retry after it goes through.
- The file keeps its existing line-ending style (LF, CRLF or CR); a new file gets LF.
- The previous state is snapshotted first, so `undo` brings it back (undoing a create deletes the file).
- After the write the file is parsed locally and syntax errors the write introduced are reported as a warning.
- Edit existing files with `patch`; use this only for new files or a deliberate full rewrite. Do not create documentation files unless asked.

